A||||4||rcn491||EGR Solenoid Repair||||1
Z||000000||rcn491||10-15-1999||01:38 AM||||If anyone is getting a EGR computer code 32 and it appears the problem is with the EGR solenoid, I found out that is possible to take it apart and clean the metal disc that acts as a valve. You need to carefully bend the metal tabs that hold the end that the vac. lines attach and remove from the rest of the unit. There is a metal disc and small spring inside. Clean and reassemble and bend the tabs back over.||||reg||1
Z||000001||GWMORGAN||10-15-1999||10:16 AM||||rcn491,<BR>      What (solvent,cleaning agent) did you use? ||||reg||1
Z||000002||rcn491||10-15-1999||09:18 PM||||I used lacquer thinner but anything will work. The stuff on mine was a fine dust that got past the filter on the other end.||||reg||1
Z||000003||pollock||10-18-1999||11:09 AM||||Thanks, man....<P>I've been staring at that engine light forever, not wanting to blow the money on that part.<P>I'll try it this week.||||reg||6
Z||000004||Linx||10-18-1999||03:40 PM||||man- I've decided that when it came to the design of the EGR solenoid, someone wasnt paying attention.<P>I've recently replaced my own, and since it was bad (the coil had opened), I decided to take it apart and have a look-see.  I'll be darned if it hadnt rusted clean through.<P>Not a month later, Im pokin' around under the hood (again!), and doggone it!  The new one is already showin' signs of water penetration, too!<P>My recommendation; coat the joint with rtv, to seal and protect it from moisture.<P>In the mean time, I'm gonna try to invent a way to use a newer (and cheaper) solenoid in it's place.||||reg||1
